---
import RealReturnsChart from '../../components/RealReturnsChart.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { fxNote, type Currency } from '../../lib/currency';
import { realReturnsFor } from '../../lib/data';
import { currencyPaths } from '../../lib/routes';
import { daysBetween, monthLabel, realTiles, sourceNameOf } from '../../lib/real-shared';

export const getStaticPaths = currencyPaths;

const { currency } = Astro.props as { currency: Currency };
const dataset = realReturnsFor(currency);

// Hoisted out of the prose: an interpolation at the start of a line loses the
// space before it, which the performance page's comment records the hard way.
const tiles = dataset ? realTiles(dataset) : [];
const deflator = dataset?.deflator;
const baseLabel = deflator ? monthLabel(deflator.baseMonth) : '';
const holes = deflator?.missingMonths ?? [];
const holeList = holes.map(monthLabel).join(', ');
const behindDays = dataset ? daysBetween(dataset.asOf, dataset.pricesThrough) : 0;
const adjustment =
  deflator?.seasonalAdjustment === 'seasonally-adjusted'
    ? 'seasonally adjusted'
    : 'not seasonally adjusted';
// Every one of these is read from the file rather than from the code that wrote
// it, including the staleness threshold. The page describes a *committed*
// dataset, which may have been produced under a different threshold than the one
// this build happens to compile — importing the constant would state today's rule
// over yesterday's figures, which is the inverse of the discipline this page is
// built on.
const sourceName = deflator ? sourceNameOf(deflator.source) : '';
const maxLag = deflator?.maxLagMonths ?? 0;
---

<Base
  currency={currency}
  title="blockplot · real returns"
  description="BTC's price as it was quoted and restated in constant money, with nominal and inflation-adjusted returns side by side."
>
  <h1 class="visually-hidden">Real returns</h1>

  {
    dataset && deflator ? (
      <>
        <dl class="stat-grid">
          {tiles.map((tile) => (
            <div class="stat">
              <dt>{tile.label}</dt>
              <dd class={`num ${tile.tone}`}>{tile.value}</dd>
              <dd class="sub num">{tile.sub}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>

        <section class="chart-section">
          {/* No range or scale in the heading — the chart's own controls carry
              that state. The base month is not here either: it moves once a
              month and the caption under the chart is its one home. */}
          <h2>BTC as quoted, and in constant money</h2>
          <RealReturnsChart currency={currency} dataset={dataset} />
          <p class="method-note">
            Each day's price is multiplied by the ratio of two CPI observations — the base month's
            over that day's — so the real line reads as {baseLabel} money throughout. Because both
            terms are observations of the same index, its base period cancels exactly: it makes no
            difference that {deflator.sourceSeries} is published on one base and another country's
            index on another, and no rescaling is done or needed. The tiles above lead with the real
            figure and carry the nominal one underneath, which is the opposite of the usual order
            and the point of the page: every other page here quotes nominal figures.
          </p>
          <p class="method-note">
            The deflator is {deflator.sourceSeries} from {sourceName}, {adjustment}, published
            monthly from {monthLabel(deflator.firstMonth)} through {monthLabel(deflator.lastMonth)}. A
            month's observation applies to every day in that month and is never carried into the
            next one. Carrying it forward is the obvious way to cover the publication lag and the
            wrong one: it would deflate the most recent weeks by an index that predates them, so the
            newest points — the ones you look at first — would be the least real, with nothing on
            the page able to say by how much.
          </p>
          <p class="method-note">
            {behindDays > 0 ? (
              <>
                So both lines stop at {dataset.asOf}, {behindDays} days behind the last price
                ({dataset.pricesThrough}), and every window above is measured to that same day.
                That costs the page a few weeks of currency and buys the only thing it is for: a
                nominal figure and a real figure you can subtract. Ending nominal at today and real
                a month earlier would put a different market move in each column and label the
                difference inflation.
              </>
            ) : (
              <>
                The deflator currently reaches the last price day ({dataset.asOf}), so both lines
                and every window above end there.
              </>
            )}
            {' '}A deflator more than {maxLag} months behind is treated as retired rather than
            late, and the pipeline then writes no dataset at all rather than deflate by an index
            that has stopped moving — statistical agencies do retire series, and a retired one goes
            on being served under the same id with no error, so the alternative is a confident
            chart of stale money. The deflator is chosen from a short list in order of preference
            and the first that is both readable and current wins, so the series named above is what
            actually answered rather than what was intended.
          </p>
          {holes.length > 0 && (
            <p class="method-note">
              {holes.length === 1 ? 'One month in' : `${holes.length} months in`} the period this
              file covers {holes.length === 1 ? 'has' : 'have'} no observation: {holeList}. A short
              enough range may not reach back to {holes.length === 1 ? 'it' : 'them'}. Days in
              {holes.length === 1 ? ' it' : ' them'} are dropped rather than deflated by a
              neighbouring month, and both lines are broken there rather than drawn straight across
              — a line joined over the hole would show a deflated price for every day of a month
              the deflator does not cover. This is the source's own gap, not a fetch that failed.
              {holes.includes('2025-10') && deflator.source === 'fred' && (
                <> The October 2025 US release was cancelled rather than delayed.</>
              )}
            </p>
          )}
          <p class="method-note">
            Annualised rates appear only on windows of at least {dataset.minAnnualiseDays} days — a
            calendar year, less the few days a window may be anchored late by. Below that an
            annualised figure is an extrapolation rather than a measurement. Returns are price returns: no
            dividends, no yield, no fees, no taxes. Resolution matches the other history charts —
            the last {dataset.dailyDays} days daily, everything older one point per ISO week, its
            last close rather than an average. CPI is revised after first publication and this page
            always shows the latest vintage, so a figure here can move slightly without any price
            having changed.{fxNote(currency)}
          </p>
        </section>
      </>
    ) : (
      <section class="chart-section">
        <h2>Real returns are not available</h2>
        <p class="method-note">
          This page needs a consumer price index for {currency.toUpperCase()} that is still being
          published. No candidate series produced one on the last pipeline run — either none could
          be reached and read, or what could be read had stopped publishing — so no dataset was
          written rather than deflate by an index that is not moving. The pipeline log names which
          candidates were tried and why each was rejected. Nominal figures are unaffected and are
          on every other page.
        </p>
      </section>
    )
  }
</Base>
